course: dictionary
lesson: 5860
title: Words that belong to a family or a place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one family, one region or one time in history. Mammy is a child's word for mother. It is informal, so you use it at home or in friendly speech, not in a formal essay. Context shows you what it means.
explain: Read this: «One day Gracie runs in and calls Granny Mammy by accident.» The clue is by accident. Gracie did not mean to call Granny that. So Mammy is a name a child uses for a mum, and she slipped into using it.
explain: Where a word is used matters. Mammy is common in Ireland, the north of England and Scotland. In a story set there, a child calling «Mammy!» shows a warm, homely family voice. In a history lesson, the same word can be an example of an unfair stereotype, so the context changes its meaning and its use.
fact: Mammy means mother. It is a child's word for mum, used in Ireland, the north of England and Scotland.
fact: Mammy is informal: you use it at home or with family, not in formal writing.
fact: Adults in Ireland may still say «my mammy» about their own mother.
fact: In history, Mammy was an offensive, racist stereotype for a Black woman who cared for white families' children in the American South. It is not used today except when talking about that history.
exercise: Gracie calls Granny Mammy by accident. What does Mammy mean here? | A place to play | *A child's word for mother | A type of dog | A cake made with butter
exercise: A child in a story says, «Mammy, can I have a drink?» Who is the child talking to? | A bus driver | A shopkeeper | *Their mum | A teacher
exercise: In a history lesson, why is Mammy discussed? | It was the name of a famous queen | It is a polite name for a grandmother | *It was an offensive stereotype, so it is not a word to use today | It means a type of bread
test: Is mammy formal or informal? | *Informal | Scientific | Technical | Formal
test: Which word is closest in meaning to mammy, in the first sense? | Father | Teacher | Dog | *Mum
exam: Why is it important to know that mammy was once an offensive stereotype? | To learn a new spelling | *To understand the history and never use it as a friendly word | To say it louder | To use it when writing invitations
